Written-by: ScienceDiscovery Product
Claimed-by: ScienceDiscovery Product
Claim-Id: df196a9b36e24084ad2fcd7ec43213c4
Branch: dev/665-bug-web-fetch-public-url-guard-misses-na
Base: 80ff50c18cdf
At: 2026-10-29T04:18:18Z

Implementation: ScienceDiscovery (ours) — do not pick up without a `pickup-ok` comment.

The public-URL gate for `web_fetch` now judges IPv4-mapped (`::ffff:a.b.c.d`) and IPv4-compatible (`::a.b.c.d`) literals by their embedded IPv4 (#662 / PR #664). Three other IPv6 forms that also carry an IPv4 address still pass, so the same model-supplied URL can reach loopback or the link-local metadata endpoint through them.

Verified on live main `a074172f60e5667e72ff04246316a0a41552cb46`, `packages/data-source/src/web/native/url-guard.ts`:

- NAT64 well-known prefix `64:ff9b::/96` — the IPv4 address sits in the last two hextets. `ipv6Blocked` only consults `ipv4Blocked` for the mapped/compatible forms (`url-guard.ts:58-68` on main; `:81-97` after #664), so `64:ff9b::...` falls to `return false` (`:68` on main, `:97` after #664).
- 6to4 `2002::/16` — the IPv4 address sits in hextets 2–3, which no branch inspects.
- IPv4-translated `::ffff:0:0:0/96` — 80 zero bits, then `ffff`, then 16 zero bits, then the IPv4 address; it matches neither the mapped test (`hextets[5] === 0xffff`) nor the compatible test (first six hextets zero) that #664 added at `url-guard.ts:95-97`.
- Deprecated site-local `fec0::/10` — the link-local branch tests `fe8`–`feb` only, so `fec0::/10` is reported public.

Current vs expected: `isBlockedAddress("64:ff9b::7f00:1")`, `"2002:7f00:1::"`, `"::ffff:0:7f00:1"` and `"fec0::1"` all return `false`; each must return `true`, and `assertPublicUrl("http://[64:ff9b::7f00:1]/")` and the two siblings must reject with `local and private network URLs are not allowed`. Decoding must stay narrow: `64:ff9b::808:808`, `2002:808:808::` and `::ffff:0:808:808` embed the public `8.8.8.8` and must keep returning `false` — a fix that rejects the whole prefix breaks NAT64 and 6to4 egress.

Suggested red test, in `packages/data-source/src/web/native/providers.test.ts`, next to `web_fetch refuses private and loopback targets`:

```ts
test("web_fetch judges NAT64, 6to4 and IPv4-translated literals by their embedded IPv4", () => {
  for (const address of ["64:ff9b::7f00:1", "2002:7f00:1::", "::ffff:0:7f00:1", "fec0::1"]) {
    assert.equal(isBlockedAddress(address), true, address);
  }
  for (const address of ["64:ff9b::808:808", "2002:808:808::", "::ffff:0:808:808"]) {
    assert.equal(isBlockedAddress(address), false, address);
  }
});
```

Run: `cd packages/data-source && node --import tsx --test src/web/native/providers.test.ts`

Current result on main: 8 of 12 rows red (`false !== true` on the four blocked rows and on the three URL forms; the three public rows pass). Dedupe: open + closed search for `NAT64`, `64:ff9b`, `6to4`, `2002::`, `fec0`, `site-local`, `IPv4-translated` returns only #662/#664, which scope these classes out — no separate issue exists.

Scope note: this is the follow-up the #664 body lists as out of scope. One PR, one class of change: decode the embedded IPv4 for each of these prefixes instead of blocking the prefix.

issue:706.body:
### What is wrong

`CasStore.read()` walks three candidate paths but drops out on the first one that fails an
integrity re-hash, so a recoverable object is reported as lost.

`packages/cas/src/index.ts:140-145` — the three layouts it is meant to search:

```ts
private readPaths(hash: string): string[] {
  const current = this.objectPath(hash); // validates before resolving any path
  const other = this.pool === "data" ? "agent-state" : "data";
  return [current, resolve(this.dataDir, "versioning", other, "blobs", "sha256", hash),
    resolve(this.dataDir, "cas", "sha256", hash.slice(0, 2), hash)];
}
```

`packages/cas/src/index.ts:94-105` — the loop that stops at the first corrupt copy:

```ts
async read(hash: string): Promise<Buffer> {
  for (const path of this.readPaths(hash)) {
    try {
      const bytes = await readFile(path);
      if (sha256(bytes) !== hash) throw new Error("CAS integrity failure");
      return bytes;
    } catch (error) {
      if ((error as NodeJS.ErrnoException).code !== "ENOENT") throw error;
    }
  }
  throw Object.assign(new Error("CAS object not found"), { code: "ENOENT" });
}
```

The thrown `CAS integrity failure` carries no `code`, so the `catch` at `:101` re-throws it and
paths 2 and 3 are never read. A corrupt copy in one layout therefore hides an intact copy in
the next one. This contradicts the documented contract in `docs/en/developer-docs/cas.md`
("Its legacy bare-hash read interface searches both pools and the previous
`cas/sha256/<first-two>/<hash>` layout"), and `stream()` (`:108-124`) and `retain()` (`:127-138`)
have the same first-path-wins shape.

### What it costs at the API surface

`CAS blob missing for this content_hash` occurs exactly once in the tree at that tip (code
search), in the handler at `services/api/src/http/index.ts:2938-2994` (`GET /api/sessions/<session>/web-pages/<webPage>/content`):

```ts
const dataCas = new CasStore(store.dataDir, "data");
if (!(await dataCas.has(contentHash))) {
  return sendError(response, 404, "CAS blob missing for this content_hash");
}
const bytes = await dataCas.read(contentHash);
```

with the loud branch further down (`:2986-2992`):

```ts
} catch (error) {
  apiLog.warn("web_page_content_failed", { ... });
  return sendError(response, 500, "WebPage content read failed");
}
```

So a corrupt pool copy plus an intact legacy copy ends at `:2969` as **404 "CAS blob missing"**
while the bytes are in fact readable from the legacy path — and, once `has()` answers `false`
for corruption (see below), **no `apiLog.warn` fires**: the corruption becomes indistinguishable
from "the object was never stored".

### Why this is not #680

`#680` (`fix(cas): answer has() false for a corrupted object, like verify()`) changes the
predicate `has()`: today it re-throws the integrity error (`packages/cas/src/index.ts:74-82`),
and the fix maps it to `false` the way `verify()` already does (`:84-92`). That is correct for
`has()`'s boolean contract, but on its own it makes this endpoint **quieter**: `has()` →
`false` → the `:2969` 404 returns without reaching the `:2986` `apiLog.warn`. This issue is
about `read()` — availability and the fallback order — not about the boolean predicate, and it
needs its own fix so the two changes together still fail loudly. Filing them separately is
deliberate; the sequencing note matters more than the file split.

### Expected

1. **The legacy layout is actually read.** If any candidate path contains bytes whose SHA-256
   equals the requested hash, `read()` returns them. A fix that only turns the 404 into a 500
   without trying the remaining `readPaths` entries still treats a recoverable object as lost
   and fails this criterion.
2. **Total loss stays loud.** If every candidate exists but none matches its digest, `read()`
   throws `CAS integrity failure`, the endpoint answers 500 (not 404), and `apiLog.warn`
   fires — corruption must show up as corruption, not as "blob missing".
3. **A genuinely missing object is unchanged.** All candidates `ENOENT` → `read()` throws the
   `ENOENT`-coded `CAS object not found`, the endpoint answers 404.

### Acceptance probes

- (a) Corrupt the `data`-pool copy of a stored blob, keep an intact legacy `cas/sha256/xx/<hash>`
  copy → `read(hash)` returns the original bytes and `GET .../content` serves 200 with them.
- (b) Corrupt every candidate copy of the blob → `read(hash)` throws `CAS integrity failure`,
  the endpoint answers 500, and `web_page_content_failed` is logged.
- (c) Ask for a hash that was never stored → `read()` throws `CAS object not found` with
  `code: "ENOENT"`, the endpoint answers 404, no warn.

### Suggested fix shape (for the implementer to check, not a prescription)

```ts
let corrupted = false;
for (const path of this.readPaths(hash)) {
  try {
    const bytes = await readFile(path);
    if (sha256(bytes) !== hash) throw new Error("CAS integrity failure");
    return bytes;
  } catch (error) {
    if ((error as Error).message === "CAS integrity failure") { corrupted = true; continue; }
    if ((error as NodeJS.ErrnoException).code !== "ENOENT") throw error;
  }
}
if (corrupted) throw new Error("CAS integrity failure");
throw Object.assign(new Error("CAS object not found"), { code: "ENOENT" });
```

Optional hardening worth deciding in review: give integrity errors a stable
`code: "EINTEGRITY"` and test the code instead of the message string at `:89`, `:98`, and the
new sites. `stream()` and `retain()` should follow the same fallback rule for consistency.

### Out of scope

- `#680`'s `has()` predicate change (separate PR, already open).
- Any other truncation/`slice` point in `packages/data-source` (unrelated).

### Environment

main `3fe9f9c9fca9e5c26ab8c53a3c15666823d3ab02`; `packages/cas/src/index.ts` blob `584cf1ea`;
`services/api/src/http/index.ts` blob `d92e98fe`. Line numbers are from those two blobs.

### Claim

- Branch: `dev2/fix/cas-read-legacy-fallback` (fork `HugoXK/sciencediscovery`), base `3fe9f9c9fca9e5c26ab8c53a3c15666823d3ab02`.
- First push: a failing test that corrupts the current-pool copy while an intact copy sits in the legacy layout.
- Owner: ScienceDiscovery Dev-2; reviewer request follows the repository CODEOWNERS on the PR.

issue:783.body:
Implementation: ScienceDiscovery (ours) — PR follows within 24h. Do not pick up without a `pickup-ok` comment on this issue.

Written-by: ScienceDiscovery Product
Claimed-by: ScienceDiscovery Product
Claim-Id: 81b377db505b4720a7e42d7b8ac02a06
Branch: dev2/fix/783-usage-cache-cost
Base: a429ba1d4eca17af8cfabb9f28abe9d651e38e6c
At: 2026-11-03T04:58:53Z

## Summary

For Chat Completions (OpenAI-compatible, including DeepSeek) models the Usage page's estimated cost charges the cached part of the prompt **twice**: `inputTokens` is `prompt_tokens`, which already contains `prompt_cache_hit_tokens`, and `estimateInvocationCost` then adds `cacheReadTokens` on top at the cached rate.

A call with 1,000,000 prompt tokens of which 800,000 were cache hits is estimated at **2.804 CNY** where the provider bills **0.804 CNY** — a 3.49x overestimate. With no published cached rate the same call is estimated at 4.004 CNY against 2.004 CNY.

Anthropic Messages usage (`input_tokens` excludes `cache_read_input_tokens`) is estimated correctly, which is exactly the asymmetry that locates the defect.

## Reproduce on main `a429ba1d4eca17af8cfabb9f28abe9d651e38e6c`

```bash
cd <repo-checkout-on-main> && cat > cache-cost-probe.ts <<'EOF'
import { normalizeUsage } from "./packages/model/src/usage.ts";
import { parseProviderUsage } from "./services/api/src/provider-usage.ts";
import { estimateInvocationCost } from "./services/api/src/model-usage.ts";

// DeepSeek Chat Completions usage: prompt_tokens = cache hit + cache miss.
const wire = {
  completion_tokens: 500,
  prompt_cache_hit_tokens: 800_000,
  prompt_cache_miss_tokens: 200_000,
  prompt_tokens: 1_000_000,
  total_tokens: 1_000_500,
};
console.log("normalizeUsage  :", JSON.stringify(normalizeUsage(wire, "openai-chat-completions")));
const recorded = parseProviderUsage(wire);
console.log("recorded usage  :", JSON.stringify({
  cacheReadTokens: recorded.cacheReadTokens, cacheWriteTokens: recorded.cacheWriteTokens,
  inputTokens: recorded.inputTokens, outputTokens: recorded.outputTokens, totalTokens: recorded.totalTokens,
}));

const invocation = {
  cacheReadTokens: recorded.cacheReadTokens, cacheWriteTokens: recorded.cacheWriteTokens, costUsd: null,
  id: "probe", inputTokens: recorded.inputTokens, modelProfileId: "deepseek-chat",
  modelProfileName: "DeepSeek Chat", outputTokens: recorded.outputTokens, projectId: undefined,
  sessionId: "s", startedAt: "2026-11-03T00:00:00.000Z", totalTokens: recorded.totalTokens,
  usageStatus: "reported",
} as never;
const pricing = { cachedInput: 0.5, currency: "CNY", input: 2, output: 8, unit: "per-1m-tokens" } as never;
console.log("estimated cost  :", JSON.stringify(estimateInvocationCost(invocation, pricing)));
console.log("provider charge :", (((1_000_000 - 800_000) * 2 + 800_000 * 0.5 + 500 * 8) / 1_000_000).toFixed(6), "CNY");
EOF
node_modules/.bin/tsx cache-cost-probe.ts
```

Actual output:

```
normalizeUsage  : {"inputTokens":1000000,"outputTokens":500,"totalTokens":1000500,"cacheReadTokens":800000,"cacheWriteTokens":200000}
recorded usage  : {"cacheReadTokens":800000,"cacheWriteTokens":200000,"inputTokens":1000000,"outputTokens":500,"totalTokens":1000500}
estimated cost  : {"amount":2.804,"currency":"CNY"}
provider charge : 0.804000 CNY
```

The same numbers without a published cached rate, next to an Anthropic-shaped control:

```bash
cat > cache-cost-probe2.ts <<'EOF'
import { normalizeUsage } from "./packages/model/src/usage.ts";
import { estimateInvocationCost } from "./services/api/src/model-usage.ts";
const base = { costUsd: null, id: "p", modelProfileId: "m", modelProfileName: "M", projectId: undefined,
  sessionId: "s", startedAt: "2026-11-03T00:00:00.000Z", usageStatus: "reported" } as never;
const chat = normalizeUsage({ completion_tokens: 500, prompt_cache_hit_tokens: 800_000, prompt_cache_miss_tokens: 200_000, prompt_tokens: 1_000_000, total_tokens: 1_000_500 }, "openai-chat-completions")!;
console.log("chat, no cachedInput :", JSON.stringify(estimateInvocationCost({ ...(base as object), ...chat } as never, { currency: "CNY", input: 2, output: 8, unit: "per-1m-tokens" } as never)), "| provider:", ((1_000_000 * 2 + 500 * 8) / 1e6).toFixed(6));
const anthropic = normalizeUsage({ input_tokens: 1_000_000, output_tokens: 500, cache_read_input_tokens: 800_000 }, "anthropic-messages")!;
console.log("anthropic shape      :", JSON.stringify(estimateInvocationCost({ ...(base as object), ...anthropic } as never, { cachedInput: 0.5, currency: "CNY", input: 2, output: 8, unit: "per-1m-tokens" } as never)), "| provider:", ((1_000_000 * 2 + 800_000 * 0.5 + 500 * 8) / 1e6).toFixed(6));
EOF
node_modules/.bin/tsx cache-cost-probe2.ts
```

```
chat, no cachedInput : {"amount":4.004,"currency":"CNY"} | provider: 2.004000
anthropic shape      : {"amount":2.404,"currency":"CNY"} | provider: 2.404000
```

## Why this is a defect and not a rounding choice

- Chat Completions defines `prompt_tokens` as cache hit + cache miss. This repository already reports that shape itself: `services/api/src/agent-run/jiuwenswarm-model-gateway.ts:130` sends `prompt_tokens_details.cached_tokens` alongside `prompt_tokens`, and `packages/model/src/usage.ts:34,42` keeps `prompt_tokens` as `inputTokens` for the chat dialect (`normalizeUsage(..., "openai-chat-completions")` is how streamed usage is ingested, `packages/model/src/client.ts:832`).
- Anthropic Messages puts cache reads *outside* `input_tokens` (`packages/model/src/client.ts:1211`), so adding `cacheReadTokens` there is correct.
- `services/api/src/model-usage.ts:373-376` adds both unconditionally. The existing USG-015 case (`services/api/src/model-usage.test.ts:219`) builds `ModelInvocationUsage` directly and never routes it through the Chat Completions normalizer, so it cannot see the overlap; the Anthropic-shaped result it pins (6.65) stays valid.
- `ModelInvocationUsage` (`packages/schema/src/model-usage.ts:167`) carries no dialect, so at cost time the two shapes are indistinguishable — the estimate is right for one and wrong for the other.

## Files and lines

- `services/api/src/model-usage.ts:367-382` — `estimateInvocationCost` (formula at `:373-376`), called from `summarizeModelUsageAnalytics` at `:406`.
- `packages/model/src/usage.ts:34,42,62-63` — the normalizer that knows the dialect and where `inputTokens` starts containing the cached span.
- `packages/schema/src/model-usage.ts:167` — the recorded usage shape that does not carry the dialect today.
- Tests to extend: `services/api/src/model-usage.test.ts`, and a normalizer case in `packages/model/src/usage.test.ts`.

## Expected

- Chat Completions, pricing `{input: 2, cachedInput: 0.5, output: 8}`: `((input - cacheRead) * input + cacheRead * cachedInput + output * output + cacheWrite * cacheWriteInput) / 1e6` = **0.804**, not 2.804.
- Chat Completions with no `cachedInput` published: the cached span is still inside `inputTokens`, so **2.0008**, not 4.004.
- Anthropic Messages: unchanged, 2.404.

## Acceptance criteria

1. New `services/api/src/model-usage.test.ts` cases feed usage produced by `normalizeUsage(..., "openai-chat-completions")` and assert 0.804 (with `cachedInput`) and 2.0008 (without) for the payload above.
2. An Anthropic-shaped record (`input_tokens` 1,000,000 + `cache_read_input_tokens` 800,000) still estimates 2.404, and the existing USG-015 6.65 assertion is unchanged.
3. The estimate for a dialect whose prompt already contains the cached span never charges that span at both rates — i.e. the result never exceeds `input * inputRate + output * outputRate + cacheWrite * cacheWriteRate` for such a record.
4. `packages/model` (136 tests today) and the `services/api` usage/analytics tests stay green.

## Scope

- Smallest slice: keep the dialect (or a `cachedTokensIncludedInInput` flag) from `normalizeUsage` on the recorded usage, and subtract the cached span before applying `pricing.input` in `estimateInvocationCost`. No UI change is required; the Usage page reads the summary.
- Out of scope: the `prompt_cache_miss_tokens` -> `cacheWriteTokens` mapping at `packages/model/src/usage.ts:63`, which USG-014 (`services/api/src/provider-usage.test.ts:34`) pins deliberately.
- Dedupe: searched issues and pull requests for cached-token cost, estimated cost, `prompt_cache_hit_tokens`, `USG-015`, and open PRs on `model-usage.ts` / `usage.ts`; nothing covers this. Open PR #304 reworks protocol token parsing and does not touch cost estimation.

## Notes

- Evidence only; no implementation in this issue. Lane: API/services + packages (Dev-2).

issue:788.body:
Implementation: ScienceDiscovery (ours) — PR follows within 24h. Do not pick up without a `pickup-ok` comment on this issue.

Written-by: ScienceDiscovery Product
Claimed-by: ScienceDiscovery Product
Claim-Id: af4320ea466941a49524cfbc97abacde
Branch: dev2/fix/788-download-total-bytes
Base: a429ba1d4eca17af8cfabb9f28abe9d651e38e6c
At: 2026-11-03T05:31:33Z

## What is wrong

A governed artifact download from a source that answers with a **chunked body (no `Content-Length`)** is recorded as a **0-byte** file: progress jumps to **100%** after the first megabyte and stays there while the rest of the file is still arriving.

`parseTotalBytes` asks for the header and feeds it straight to `Number`:

```ts
// packages/artifact-manager/src/governed-download-manager.ts:111-117
function parseTotalBytes(response: Response, offset: number): number | undefined {
  const contentRange = response.headers.get("content-range");
  const rangeTotal = contentRange?.match(/\/(\d+)$/)?.[1];
  if (rangeTotal) return Number(rangeTotal);
  const contentLength = Number(response.headers.get("content-length"));   // :115
  return Number.isFinite(contentLength) && contentLength >= 0 ? offset + contentLength : undefined;
}
```

`Headers.get()` returns `null` when the header is absent, and `Number(null)` is `0`, so the "unknown" case is parsed as "zero bytes". Downstream:

- `:584` `const totalBytes = candidate.expectedBytes ?? parseTotalBytes(response, offset);` → `0`
- `:684` `percent: totalBytes ? … : 100` → `100`, stored with `totalBytes: 0` for a job that is still `running`
- `apps/web/src/GovernedDownloadCards.tsx:151` renders `{job.progress.percent ?? 0}%` → **"Running · 100% · 1,048,576 bytes"** while half the file has arrived
- the completed job keeps `totalBytes: 0` (`:645-650`), so every consumer of the durable record sees a 0-byte artifact

This is the normal path for the product's own sources, not an exotic one: no in-repo MCP source sets `expectedBytes` on a candidate (`services/gateway/src/sciencediscovery_gateway/public_biomed_mcp.py:360-371`, `:386-397`, `:513-529` emit no size and no checksum), so `parseTotalBytes` is the only size signal a governed download has. With `totalBytes = 0` the pre-flight quota check at `:588` (`totalBytes > artifactLimit`) is also skipped; only the streaming counter still aborts a runaway body.

## Reproduction

On live `main` `a429ba1d4eca17af8cfabb9f28abe9d651e38e6c`, Node v22.23.2. The probe is `services/api/src/mcp/governed-download-manager.test.ts` with one added case: an appended plan/candidate **without `expectedBytes`** and a fake `fetch` returning `new Response(stream)` with no `content-length` (two 1 MiB chunks, 600 ms apart), driven through `resumeInterrupted()`:

```bash
cd services/api && node --import tsx --test src/mcp/governed-download-manager.test.ts   # with the case below
```

Observed on main (the probe prints the persisted job at half and at the end):

```text
MID-FLIGHT {"bytesDownloaded":1048576,"filesCompleted":0,"filesTotal":1,"percent":100,"totalBytes":0}
TERMINAL   {"bytesDownloaded":2097152,"filesCompleted":1,"filesTotal":1,"percent":100,"totalBytes":0}
```

A real chunked HTTP response behaves the same way (same box):

```text
$ node -e 'http server writes 1 KiB, waits, writes 1 KiB, ends; fetch it'
chunked content-length header: null
Number(null) -> 0
body bytes: 2048
```

The test case (same `store` / `registry` / `broker` setup as the "resumes concurrent downloads" case in that file; the candidate has no `expectedBytes` and no `checksum`):

```ts
test("governed download of a chunked response reports no total and never claims 100% early", async (context) => {
  const chunk = Buffer.alloc(1024 * 1024, 0x61);
  await store.appendArtifactPlan(plan);   // candidate: kind "dataset", format "csv", sourceUrl on the pubmed allowlist
  await store.appendArtifactJob(job);     // state "queued", progress { bytesDownloaded: 0, filesCompleted: 0, filesTotal: 1 }
  const manager = new GovernedDownloadManager(store, registry, broker, async () => new Response(
    new ReadableStream<Uint8Array>({
      async start(controller) {
        controller.enqueue(new Uint8Array(chunk));
        await new Promise((wait) => setTimeout(wait, 600));
        controller.enqueue(new Uint8Array(chunk));
        controller.close();
      },
    }),
    { status: 200, headers: { "content-type": "text/csv" } },   // no content-length
  ), 10 * 1024 * 1024);
  await manager.resumeInterrupted();
  // poll the persisted job until it is running with bytes on disk, then wait for the terminal state
  assert.equal(running.progress.totalBytes, undefined);    // actual: 0
  assert.equal(running.progress.percent, undefined);       // actual: 100
  assert.equal(completed.progress.totalBytes, undefined);  // actual: 0
});
```

Expected: while the total is unknown the job carries **no** `percent` and **no** `totalBytes` (the same shape `createJob` produces at `:418-423` when `expectedBytes` is absent), so the card shows bytes only; a known total still yields the real percentage.

## Minimal fix

Treat an absent header as unknown instead of zero, and leave the rest of the state machine alone:

```ts
const header = response.headers.get("content-length");
const contentLength = header === null ? Number.NaN : Number(header);
return Number.isFinite(contentLength) && contentLength >= 0 ? offset + contentLength : undefined;
```

A `content-length: 0` response therefore still reports `totalBytes: 0` (the empty-file case), and `content-range` / `expectedBytes` keep their precedence.

## Acceptance

- New test in `services/api/src/mcp/governed-download-manager.test.ts` (`governed download of a chunked response reports no total and never claims 100% early`): mid-flight job has `progress.totalBytes === undefined` and `"percent" in progress === false`; completed job has `bytesDownloaded === 2 * 1024 * 1024`, `state === "completed"`, `progress.totalBytes === undefined`.
- The existing cases in that file stay green, including the `content-length` path and the interrupted-resume case.
- `cd services/api && node --import tsx --test src/mcp/governed-download-manager.test.ts` is green; `pnpm -C packages/artifact-manager build` (or the repo's package build) succeeds.

Files: `packages/artifact-manager/src/governed-download-manager.ts:111-117` (fix), `services/api/src/mcp/governed-download-manager.test.ts` (regression test). Lane: Dev-2 (shared package + API test).

## Dedupe

No open issue or PR mentions `parseTotalBytes`, "totalBytes" or download progress: GitHub search `repo:openJiuwen-ai/sciencediscovery` gives 0 hits for `parseTotalBytes` and `totalBytes`, 0 for `"content-length" download progress`; `governed-download-manager` matches only unrelated PRs (#441, #311) and closed #445 (approval discoverability). Not the #783 usage-cost or #787 ChEMBL work.

Reproduced red before filing.

issue:795.body:
## What happens

A JSON artifact that starts with a UTF-8 BOM is reported as invalid. The dashboard strips exactly one leading `U+FEFF` on the delimited-text path but not on the JSON path:

```ts
// services/api/src/artifact-dashboard.ts:260-262  (CSV/TSV path)
// Strip exactly one U+FEFF, and only at the start of the file: a U+FEFF
// anywhere else is ordinary data.
const source = rawSource.startsWith("\uFEFF") ? rawSource.slice(1) : rawSource;
```

```ts
// services/api/src/artifact-dashboard.ts:410-415  (JSON path)
let parsed: unknown;
let valid = true;
try {
  parsed = JSON.parse(source) as unknown;
} catch {
  valid = false;
}
```

`JSON.parse` rejects a leading BOM, so `valid` is false, the preview falls through to `json-source` with `parsed: false`, and the user sees `artifact.invalidJsonNote` — "This file is not valid JSON; the raw text is shown below." — for a file that is valid JSON. The record array is no longer offered as a table either.

This is what Windows Notepad and PowerShell `Out-File -Encoding utf8` write for `.json`, so it is the common case for artifacts produced on Windows, not an exotic one.

## Expected

The JSON preview applies the same rule as the delimited path: strip exactly one leading `U+FEFF`, never one in the middle. A BOM-prefixed JSON record array previews as `dataset-table`; a BOM-prefixed JSON scalar document previews as `json-source` with `parsed: true`; the raw text shown keeps the document's own characters (the stripped BOM is not content).

## Evidence

Reproduced on `main` `99298fe7cc6aba54181b93cd071d5357cd0073a0` through `buildArtifactPreview` with the bytes `EF BB BF [{...}]`:

```
# BOM-JSON {"mode":"json-source","parsed":false}
not ok 1 - a JSON document saved with a UTF-8 BOM still previews as a table
# tests 1
# pass 0
# fail 1
```

The same record array without the BOM already previews as `dataset-table`, so the only difference is the three prefix bytes.

## Acceptance

- A BOM-prefixed JSON record array previews as `dataset-table` with the same columns and rows as the same bytes without the BOM.
- A BOM-prefixed JSON scalar object previews as `json-source` with `parsed: true`; a document with a `U+FEFF` inside a string keeps it.
- Existing `artifact-dashboard` tests stay green, including the delimited-text BOM case from #654.

issue:816.body:
## Problem

On native Windows, a normal chat run can terminate the API process when `SessionStore.writeArray` cannot rename a temporary state file over an existing `session-runs/<session-id>.json`. The rejected persistence operation escapes and Node exits; clients then receive `ECONNREFUSED`, and the stack supervisor cleans up the remaining services.

Confirmed by running **upstream main**, not only a downstream branch:
- Commit: `eae2cd354a366dab3796f46c9da8fe5dd7aff11c`.
- Windows 11 Pro, build 26200, x64; native Node.js 22.23.4; pnpm 11.1.2.
- Clean detached worktree; independent `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` and `pnpm build`; no product-source or lockfile changes. Startup reported Runner build `eae2cd35`.
- Native executor, real API and Runner, real managed Bash execution; a deterministic local OpenAI-compatible SSE server supplies model tool calls. No paid model, mocked filesystem, artificial lock holder, or fault injection.
- Existing managed environment and offline package cache were reused in an isolated data directory. No user data was modified.

## Reproduction

1. Check out the commit above in a native Windows checkout, install locked dependencies, and build.
2. Prepare a working managed Python/Bash starter environment through product setup. Use isolated data and ports. Existing local package caches can be reused.
3. Start the product with `node scripts/start-stack.mjs --mode local --no-build --no-jiuwenswarm`. For the client below, use API port 24410 and free model-stub port 24420; point `SCIENCE_AGENT_RUNNER_URL` at the matching isolated Runner port. Set a test-owned `SCIENCE_AGENT_AUTH_TOKEN`.
4. Save the client below as `reproduce.mjs`, set `REPRO_TOKEN` to that same test token, then run `node reproduce.mjs`. It registers a loopback model, creates sessions, submits real chat requests, polls run state, and retrieves execution output. All filesystem operations are product operations.
5. In one continuous upstream-main attempt, **10 runs completed successfully; run 11 crashed the API**. This is timing-sensitive, not a claim that every machine will fail on run 11. The prior downstream test also encountered the same failure naturally.

<details><summary>Executable reproduction client</summary>

```js
import assert from 'node:assert/strict';
import {createServer} from 'node:http';
import {writeFile} from 'node:fs/promises';
const base='http://127.0.0.1:24410',token=process.env.REPRO_TOKEN;
const json=async(p,b)=>{const r=await fetch(base+p,{method:b?'POST':'GET',headers:{authorization:`Bearer ${token}`,'content-type':'application/json'},...(b?{body:JSON.stringify(b)}:{})});const t=await r.text();assert.ok(r.ok,`${p}: ${r.status} ${t}`);return JSON.parse(t)};
const evidence=[],steps=[];
const commands=["set -e; printf 'c\\na\\nb\\n' > shell-input.txt; printf 'BASH_PATH='; readlink /proc/$$/exe; bash --version | head -n 1; printf 'HEAD='; head -n 1 shell-input.txt; printf 'TAIL='; tail -n 1 shell-input.txt; printf 'WC='; wc -l < shell-input.txt; printf 'SORT\\n'; sort shell-input.txt; printf 'GREP='; grep b shell-input.txt; printf 'SED\\n'; sed 's/b/B/' shell-input.txt",'windows_verification_missing_command_83971'];
let scenario=0;
const stub=createServer(async(req,res)=>{try{const chunks=[];for await(const c of req)chunks.push(c);const input=JSON.parse(Buffer.concat(chunks));const msgs=input.messages,ix=msgs.findLastIndex(m=>m.role==='user'),history=msgs.slice(ix+1),tools=history.filter(m=>m.role==='tool');evidence.push({scenario,input});let call;
if(!tools.length)call={name:'run_shell',arguments:{command:commands[scenario]}};
else {const last=String(tools.at(-1).content);let parsed;try{parsed=JSON.parse(last)}catch{};const id=parsed?.id??parsed?.execution?.id;if(id&&!/COMMAND_NOT_FOUND/.test(last)&&!/(?:completed|failed)/.test(last))call={name:'execution_status',arguments:{execution_id:id,wait_ms:10000}};}
const delta=call?{role:'assistant',tool_calls:[{index:0,id:`call_${Date.now()}`,type:'function',function:{name:call.name,arguments:JSON.stringify(call.arguments)}}]}:{role:'assistant',content:'Shell results received.'};res.writeHead(200,{'content-type':'text/event-stream'});for(const[d,f]of[[delta,null],[{},call?'tool_calls':'stop']])res.write(`data: ${JSON.stringify({id:'windows',object:'chat.completion.chunk',created:1,model:'windows',choices:[{index:0,delta:d,finish_reason:f}]})}\n\n`);res.end('data: [DONE]\n\n');}catch(e){res.writeHead(500);res.end(String(e))}});
await new Promise(r=>stub.listen(24420,'127.0.0.1',r));
try{const m=await json('/api/models',{name:'Windows isolated',model:'windows',apiToken:token,baseUrl:'http://127.0.0.1:24420/v1'}),p=await json('/api/projects',{name:'Windows shell validation '+Date.now()});
for(let attempt=0;attempt<30;attempt++){for(scenario=0;scenario<commands.length;scenario++){const s=await json(`/api/projects/${p.id}/sessions`,{title:`Windows shell ${scenario}`,modelId:m.id,approvalMode:'always_allow'}),run=await json(`/api/sessions/${s.id}/runs`,{content:`Execute Windows verification scenario ${scenario}`});let terminal;
for(let i=0;i<240;i++){const r=(await json(`/api/sessions/${s.id}/runs`)).find(x=>x.id===run.id);if(['completed','failed','cancelled'].includes(r.status)){terminal=r;break}await new Promise(r=>setTimeout(r,1000))}assert.ok(terminal,'Run timeout');assert.equal(terminal.status,'completed',JSON.stringify(terminal));
const activity=await json(`/api/sessions/${s.id}/agent-activity`),records=await json(`/api/sessions/${s.id}/execution-runs`);const logs=[];for(const ex of activity.executions??[]){let cursor=0;for(let n=0;n<100;n++){const page=await json(`/api/sessions/${s.id}/agent-activity/executions/${ex.id}/logs?cursor=${cursor}`);logs.push(page);if(!page.truncated||page.nextCursor===cursor)break;cursor=page.nextCursor}};evidence.push({scenario,session:s,terminal,activity,records,logs});const output=logs.flatMap(x=>x.chunks??[]).map(x=>x.text).join('').replaceAll('\r','');
if(scenario===0){assert.ok(records.some(x=>x.exitCode===0),'successful execution required');for(const pattern of [/BASH_PATH=\/usr\/bin\/bash/,/GNU bash/,/HEAD=c/,/TAIL=b/,/WC=\s*3/,/SORT\na\nb\nc/,/GREP=b/,/SED\nc\na\nB/])assert.match(output,pattern)}steps.push({scenario,status:'PASS',runId:run.id,sessionId:s.id});}
}}finally{await writeFile('repro-evidence.json',JSON.stringify({steps,evidence},null,2));stub.closeAllConnections();stub.close()}







```

</details>

## Actual result

Observed on upstream main at 2026-11-03T13:03:01Z. Session `142e4d7f-b3a7-45dc-8468-a19e279eaa6f`, run `0ff8def7-e4a5-43d0-bf5e-c109a7b3f0a7`.

Sanitized production stack trace:

```text
Error: EPERM: operation not permitted, rename
  <data>/session-runs/.state-37512-ae6a0cf6-4653-49d3-ae81-cfccb1e4f462.tmp
  -> <data>/session-runs/142e4d7f-b3a7-45dc-8468-a19e279eaa6f.json
    at async rename (node:internal/fs/promises:784:10)
    at async SessionStore.writeArray (<checkout>/services/api/dist/store.js:1829:13)
    at async <checkout>/services/api/dist/store.js:1840:13
errno: -4048
code: EPERM
syscall: rename
Node.js v22.23.4
```

The client subsequently fails with `ECONNREFUSED`; the API is no longer listening.

## Expected result

Transient Windows state-file replacement conflicts should be handled without terminating the API. If persistence cannot recover, the affected run should report an actionable error and the service should remain available, with run state recoverable.

## Investigation and limits

`services/api/src/store.ts` implements `writeArray` as temporary-file `writeFile` followed by a direct `rename`, with temporary-file cleanup in `finally`. Its mutation queue serializes writes to one path, but the replacement has no Windows retry behavior. Upstream already provides a retrying `renameReplace` in `services/api/src/platform/os/win32.ts` (related platform work: #637); this particular persistence path does not use it.

The exact conflicting file handle has **not** been identified. Antivirus, indexing, or overlapping readers are hypotheses, not established causes. The observed facts are the real `EPERM` replacement failure and the unhandled error terminating the production API. No product fix is included in this report.

issue:829.body:
Implementation: ScienceDiscovery (ours) — PR follows within 24h. Do not pick up without a `pickup-ok` comment on this issue.

Written-by: ScienceDiscovery Lead
Claimed-by: ScienceDiscovery Lead
Claim-Id: d53c4948f4414c22bd6ba9d080cb6e3a
Branch: dev2/fix/dataset-row-width
Base: 865102a336d9
At: 2026-11-04T00:05:43Z

## What happens

The dataset (CSV/TSV) table preview does not normalise a row to the header's width, so one ragged row renders misaligned: cells land under the wrong column, or under no column at all. The JSON record-array path cannot produce this, because it builds every row by mapping over `columns` — the CSV/TSV path just slices the parsed records.

On `main` `865102a336d9ed972fd073b7eecbcf10ae79ed4c`, `services/api/src/artifact-dashboard.ts:340`:

```ts
function parseDelimitedTable(source: string, subtype: ".csv" | ".tsv", maxRows: number): {
  columns: string[];
  rows: string[][];
  totalRows: number;
} {
  const delimiter = subtype === ".csv" ? "," : "\t";
  const lines = splitDelimited(source, delimiter);
  if (!lines.length) return { columns: [], rows: [], totalRows: 0 };
  return {
    columns: lines[0] ?? [],
    rows: lines.slice(1, maxRows + 1),   // <- row width is whatever the file had
    totalRows: Math.max(0, lines.length - 1),
  };
}
```

Compare the JSON path in the same file, `recordArrayTable` (`:372`), which maps over `columns` for every row, so `row.length === columns.length` always holds there.

The renderer takes the payload at face value — `apps/web/src/ScientificArtifacts.tsx:248` renders `columns` as `<th>`, `:249` renders each row's own cells as `<td>`, with no padding or truncation on either side.

## Steps to reproduce

Any delimited artifact whose body has a different number of fields from the header line. No quoting involved — a plain extra or missing comma is enough:

```
a,b
1,2,3
```

## Expected

The preview is a rectangle: each row has exactly `columns.length` cells, so every value stays under its own header — a short row is padded with empty cells, an extra field is either kept in a cell that has no header or dropped, but the grid never shears.

## Actual

The API returns rows whose width differs from `columns`, so the table shears. Measured by calling the two functions above, copied verbatim from `bd8423b8`, with no other code involved:

```
{"name":"extra cell","columns":["a","b"],"columnCount":2,"rows":[["1","2","3"]],"rowWidths":[3],"matches":false}
{"name":"short row","columns":["a","b","c"],"columnCount":3,"rows":[["1","2"]],"rowWidths":[2],"matches":false}
{"name":"quoted comma","columns":["a","b"],"columnCount":2,"rows":[["x,y","2"]],"rowWidths":[2],"matches":true}
{"name":"tsv extra","columns":["a","b"],"columnCount":2,"rows":[["1","2","3"]],"rowWidths":[3],"matches":false}
```

(`quoted comma` is the control: correct quoting still parses to the header width, so this is width normalisation only — `#650`'s quoting fix is not in question.)

In the browser the `extra cell` row produces a `<tr>` with three `<td>` under two `<th>`, so the value `3` sits in a column that has no header and the row's other two cells no longer line up with the ones above them; `short row` leaves the third column visually empty for that row.

## Why it matters

A preview's whole job is to let a reader trust the grid: which value belongs to which column is the only information it carries. Ragged CSV is common in real exports (trailing delimiters, a note line with more fields, a manually edited row), and the failure is silent — nothing in the payload or the UI says the row was short or long.

## Scope

